From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:02:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(pdu,graphics): decode ClearCodec bands one V-bar at a
 time

The ClearCodec decoder collected a whole bands layer before painting
it: every band and, in each, a VBar for every column (16 bytes in the
WebAssembly build, 32 on 64-bit), when a V-bar that hits the V-bar
cache takes two bytes on the wire. A band's header can claim 65536
columns whatever the destination's width, and columns past it were
skipped only after all of them had been decoded and stored. A server
could make the client hold about 2 GB for a 256 MiB bands layer, the
most ZGFX lets one PDU decompress to; on 64-bit, a 32 MiB layer of
65536-column bands over a 2x2 destination held 512 MiB.

ironrdp-pdu's new decode_bands_layer_vbars iterates over a layer's
V-bars, each with its band's header and its column, decoding one at a
time, and the ClearCodec decoder resolves and paints each V-bar as it
comes: the same layer now peaks at a few kilobytes. decode_bands_layer
still collects the layer, but no longer reserves room for the columns a
band's header claims before reading its V-bars.

ironrdp-testsuite-core gets a test binary of its own, peak_heap, whose
global allocator counts the bytes each thread holds, so that tests can
bound what a decoder allocates: decoding that 32 MiB layer must peak
under 1 MiB.
